      "title": "si",
      "headword": "si",
      "romanization": "si",
      "gloss": "if — one accent away from yes, and the word yes is built out of",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b9423788974ef675",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"yes.\" (Sí.) Now the pair from the accent chapter: el and él. Same letters, one accent, two words."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Today you meet the third member of that family — and it is sí with the accent taken off."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "si — if."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin sī meant if. Spanish kept it unchanged."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And the sí you already know — yes — is that same word with a pointing particle stuck on the end: Latin sīc is sī + -ce, roughly if-so, which came to mean thus, that way, and finally yes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "si from sī — if, sí from sī + -ce — thus becomes yes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So yes is literally if, pointed at. The accent is the only thing keeping them apart on the page, and it is doing the same job it did for el and él."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the real condition",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "For something that really might happen: si + present, then present."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "No new verb form. You already have every piece:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "Si tengo tiempo, hablo español., If I have time, I speak Spanish.",
                "Si llueve, no voy., If it rains, I don't go."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both halves sit in the present, and the sentence is about something genuinely possible — today, tomorrow, whenever."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The order can flip, and the comma simply goes away:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hablo español si tengo tiempo."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
